---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import data from "../../data/processed/ecosystem.json";
import { href } from "../lib/site";
---

<Layout title="Methodology">
  <div class="page-intro">
    <div class="eyebrow">Definitions / Provenance / Limitations</div>
    <h1>Methodology</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      What is measured, how sources are selected, and where the evidence stops.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="prose">
    <details open>
      <summary>Scope & source selection</summary>
      <p>
        The current GitHub sample contains {data.repositories.length} explicitly
        selected repositories, including archived projects. It is not random or
        representative of all Mapbox developers. Selected owner/repository names
        live in <code>config/sources.json</code>; the original expanded
        selection has been preserved.
      </p>
      <p>
        GitHub window: <code>{data.window.start}</code> through{" "}
        <code>{data.window.end}</code>, inclusive. Collected{" "}
        <code>{data.collectedAt}</code>. A 90-day default bounds collection cost
        and recent activity; it is not seasonally representative. Sequential API
        calls do not form an atomic snapshot.
      </p>
      <p>
        Popularity discovery is a separate, bounded GitHub search. Its exact
        query, ordering, star/fork counts, and collection timestamp are saved.
        Stars are cumulative signals, not adoption or health. Applying
        candidates retains the explicit repository list and changes the sample;
        review coverage before comparison.
      </p>
      <a class="text-link" href={href("sources")}>
        Inspect all channel statuses and observation windows →
      </a>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>Metric definitions & CHAOSS alignment</summary>
      <p>
        Definitions reference the{" "}
        <a href="https://www.chaoss.community/kb-metrics-and-metrics-models/">
          CHAOSS metrics catalog
        </a>
        . These are aligned indicators and labeled proxies, not a claim of full
        CHAOSS conformance.
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <a href="https://www.chaoss.community/kb/metric-issues-new/">
            Issues New
          </a>
          : non-PR issue creation events inside the window, all states. GitHub’s
          issue endpoint also returns PRs, which are separated.
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="https://www.chaoss.community/kb/metric-change-requests/">
            Change Requests
          </a>
          : PR creation events in the window. No quality, acceptance, or
          unique-person inference.
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="https://www.chaoss.community/kb/metric-change-requests-duration/">
            Change Requests Duration
          </a>{" "}
          uses accepted/merged requests. Our <strong>PR closure proxy</strong>{" "}
          measures creation-to-closure hours for created-window PRs closed by
          window end, including unmerged closures. Open requests are
          right-censored, favoring completed work. This is not review duration.
          GL JS’s guide describes an internal import and Copybara workflow:
          accepted public PRs may appear closed instead of merged.
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="https://www.chaoss.community/kb/metric-time-to-first-response/">
            Time to First Response
          </a>
          : our <strong>sampled issue-comment proxy</strong> uses the newest
          configured number of non-PR issues per repository. Earliest comment
          from a non-author, non-Bot account by window end. Median uses
          responded items only; both sample and responded denominators are
          displayed. Undeclared bot accounts can remain. Reactions, events,
          discussions, reviews, and private support are excluded.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Contribution discoverability</strong>: inventory of named
          CONTRIBUTING, SUPPORT, LICENSE, and CODE_OF_CONDUCT files. A local
          qualitative proxy, not a named CHAOSS metric. README or
          organization-level guidance may exist; file presence is not usability
          or acceptance policy.
        </li>
      </ol>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>Content channels & Discord access</summary>
      <p>
        RSS/Atom collection saves linked titles, dates, and publisher names; it
        does not copy article bodies. The article source uses its own configured
        window and a bounded feed item limit. Feeds expose only retained
        entries, not publication archives. Known configured Mapbox publisher
        URLs are excluded, but other affiliation and content quality remain
        unverified.
      </p>
      <p>
        YouTube collection requires an API key and uses bounded video search
        queries, page limits, date bounds, and optional known-official channel
        exclusions. Search results are not all videos; no view-count, influence,
        or outcome claims are made. Affiliation and relevance need human review.
        No transcripts or comments are collected.
      </p>
      <p>
        Curated sources accept manually selected public links, including undated
        resources. They are clearly distinguished from automated discovery. All
        links require HTTP(S) URLs.
      </p>
      <p>
        Discord is not connected for message scraping. An invite does not confer
        API permissions. An optional import accepts publishable, non-identifying
        aggregate totals with a public source URL and exact window bounds.
        Imported labels/counts are retained; message text, IDs, and arbitrary
        extra fields are discarded.
      </p>
      <p>
        Channel windows differ. The portal displays link counts as coverage of
        configured sources, never total ecosystem size. Duplicate URLs are
        removed within a source; cross-source appearances retain provenance.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>Reproduce collection & extend sources</summary>
      <pre>npm run research:sources
npm run research:discover
npm run research:collect -- --source github
npm run research:collect -- --source articles
npm test
npm run build</pre>
      <p>
        The collector loads .env automatically. Optional{" "}
        <code>GITHUB_TOKEN</code> raises public API limits;{" "}
        <code>YOUTUBE_API_KEY</code> enables video search. Credentials stay
        server-side during collection and are never written into snapshots. The
        static website has no collector credentials.
      </p>
      <p>
        GitHub acquisition paginates issue/comment endpoints, uses a 30-page
        completeness guard, and discloses tree truncation. Requests have
        timeouts, transient retries, and bounded Retry-After delays. An
        incomplete source run retains previous evidence and reports an error.
        Missing optional credentials have a distinct status and do not become
        zero measurements.
      </p>
      <p>
        Set <code>RESEARCH_END</code> to pin the observation endpoint. A requery
        can change because GitHub records are mutable. Exact numerical
        reproduction uses committed normalized evidence and metric tests.
        Discovery and public content can change independently; preserve dated
        snapshots in Git.
      </p>
      <p>
        <code>data/raw/github.json</code> retains minimized item dates, URLs,
        and sampled comment timestamps with author/bot flags. It omits profile
        data and comment bodies. <code>data/processed/ecosystem.json</code> is
        derived display data. <code>data/raw/channels</code> stores normalized
        per-source evidence; <code>data/processed/channels.json</code> stores
        source status and content links. Editorial interpretation remains in
        page source.
      </p>
      <p>
        Extension instructions, schemas, and configuration examples:{" "}
        <code>docs/SOURCES.md</code>. See README for build and GitHub Pages
        deployment.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>Interpretation limits & human review</summary>
      <ul>
        <li>
          No inference about employee performance, organizational dysfunction,
          internal strategy, or private support quality.
        </li>
        <li>
          No identity resolution, demographic inference, contributor employer
          attribution, or maintainer ranking.
        </li>
        <li>
          Automation remains in activity totals. One person may create many
          items. Deleted, inaccessible, transferred, or moderated activity may
          be missing.
        </li>
        <li>
          No interviews or newcomer studies conducted. Proposed DevRel
          experiments remain hypotheses.
        </li>
        <li>
          One snapshot does not establish a trend. Popularity-based sample
          changes prevent naive before/after comparisons.
        </li>
        <li>
          Conference venues are real historical examples with manual approximate
          coordinates; they do not describe Mapbox’s geographic community. The
          pixelmatch demo uses a synthetic sample, not research data.
        </li>
        <li>
          Review content relevance, affiliations, licenses, and interpretation
          before presenting as the author’s conclusions.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>Technical references</summary>
      <p>
        <a href="https://docs.mapbox.com/mapbox-gl-js/guides/get-started/">
          GL JS setup
        </a>{" "}
        ·{" "}
        <a href="https://docs.mapbox.com/help/dive-deeper/access-tokens/">
          Token restrictions
        </a>{" "}
        ·{" "}
        <a href="https://docs.github.com/en/rest/search/search#search-repositories">
          GitHub discovery API
        </a>{" "}
        ·{" "}
        <a href="https://developers.google.com/youtube/v3/docs/search/list">
          YouTube search API
        </a>{" "}
        ·{" "}
        <a href="https://docs.discord.com/developers/resources/channel">
          Discord API
        </a>{" "}
        · <a href="https://github.com/mapbox/pixelmatch">pixelmatch</a>
      </p>
    </details>
  </div>
</Layout>
